  ═════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <div class="section" id="s6">
    <div class="section-header">
      <h2>6. Key Findings &amp; Policy Implications</h2>
      <p>Synthesized actionable insights from the full 2020–2026 analysis, structured for executive decision-making.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="finding">
      <div class="finding-num">1</div>
      <div class="finding-body">
        <h4>Policy Architecture Dominates Crude Price Signals</h4>
        <p>The Brent-to-retail correlation of just <strong>r = 0.26</strong> confirms that government policy architecture (subsidies, taxes) exerts a far stronger influence on consumer prices than international crude market movements. Policymakers designing stabilization mechanisms should focus on fiscal instrument calibration, not simply tracking Brent movements. Pure market pass-through, absent policy intervention, would expose consumers to 141% crude price swings (Brent ranged from $47.97 to $130.00 over the period).</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="finding">
      <div class="finding-num">2</div>
      <div class="finding-body">
        <h4>Subsidy Regimes Are Extremely Effective — But Fiscally Costly</h4>
        <p>High-subsidy countries deliver petrol at <strong>$0.71/L</strong> vs. <strong>$3.45/L</strong> in Low-subsidy nations — a <strong>79.5% dampening effect</strong>. Very High subsidy countries (Venezuela, Iran, Libya, etc.) achieve $0.15/L averages. While this shields consumers entirely from Brent volatility, the fiscal cost is enormous and unsustainable at scale, particularly for commodity-exporting states facing revenue compression during crude price downturns. The 2020 COVID shock (Brent at $53.61/bbl) illustrates the vulnerability: states with high subsidy bills saw revenue fall precisely when subsidy obligations remained fixed.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="finding">
      <div class="finding-num">3</div>
      <div class="finding-body">
        <h4>Global Price Divergence is Widening — Not Converging</h4>
        <p>The Europe–Middle East regional petrol spread grew from <strong>$1.61/L in 2020 to $2.87/L in 2026</strong> — a 78% widening. Europe and Oceania (highest-tax, lowest-subsidy) are pulling further away from Middle East and South American markets (highest-subsidy). This divergence is structural, driven by differing tax and subsidy trajectories, rather than cyclical. Policymakers relying on "natural" global price convergence as a reform rationale will find that premise weakening over time.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="finding">
      <div class="finding-num">4</div>
      <div class="finding-body">
        <h4>The 2022 Energy Crisis Was the Decisive Price Shock of the Period</h4>
        <p>Brent Crude peaked at <strong>$129.27/bbl in 2022 Q2</strong>, driving the highest average global retail petrol price year ($2.51/L vs. $1.48/L in 2020 baseline). The 2022 spike, compounded by the Ukraine-Russia energy disruption, hit Low-subsidy countries hardest — European consumers faced prices exceeding <strong>$4.06/L average</strong> in 2022. This demonstrates that Low-subsidy regimes amplify global shocks directly to consumers, requiring faster and more costly emergency policy responses (emergency tax cuts, one-time transfers) than pre-committed subsidy frameworks.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="finding">
      <div class="finding-num">5</div>
      <div class="finding-body">
        <h4>High-Income Countries Use Fuel Taxes as Fiscal Tools — Not Just Revenue Streams</h4>
        <p>High-income countries carry a <strong>34.7% average fuel tax</strong> vs. 21–23% in Middle- and Low-income countries. This gap reflects the dual function of fuel taxes in advanced economies: fiscal revenue and carbon pricing signal. However, the high-tax design means that any global crude price spike is felt acutely by consumers since the absolute price level is already elevated. Policymakers in high-income nations should consider automatic tax-band adjusters (price floors/ceilings) that dampen extreme Brent pass-through without abandoning the carbon pricing signal.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="finding">
      <div class="finding-num">6</div>
      <div class="finding-body">
        <h4>Fuel Price Volatility Is Increasing — 2026 Reached a New High</h4>
        <p>Cross-country petrol price standard deviation reached <strong>$1.70/L in 2026</strong> (Q1), the highest annual figure in the dataset, exceeding 2022 crisis levels ($1.63). This rising volatility reflects structural divergence between highly subsidized states and fully market-linked economies. New entrants to subsidy reform face a widening gap between their domestic prices and global market norms, increasing the political difficulty of subsidy removal. Reform windows should be exploited during periods of falling crude prices (e.g., 2020, 2023) rather than price peaks.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="finding">
      <div class="finding-num">7</div>
      <div class="finding-body">
        <h4>Equity Considerations — Low-Income Countries Pay Less Nominally, But More Relatively</h4>
        <p>Low-income countries pay $1.39/L on average — nominally cheaper than the $3.55/L in high-income nations. However, given that per-capita income in Low-income countries may be 20–50× lower, the real affordability burden is far heavier. The 22.9% average tax rate in Low-income countries, applied to already constrained household budgets, can represent a regressive fiscal burden. Targeted fuel vouchers or income-linked transfer mechanisms are preferable to universal untargeted subsidies (which disproportionately benefit vehicle-owning wealthier households within low-income countries).</p>
      </div>
    </div>

